  "name": "rosetta-anonymous-recursion",
  "snippet": false,
  "code": "use IO.Std.write_line;\n\n// The negative argument is checked once, on entry. `rec` is how a function literal refers to\n// itself, so the recursion that follows needs no helper function and no name at all: the literal\n// is applied where it is written.\nlet fibonacci = n =>\n    if n < 0 then\n        throw System.ArgumentException(\"fibonacci is not defined for a negative argument\")\n    else\n        (i rec => if i < 2 then i else rec(i - 1) + rec(i - 2) fi)(n)\n    fi;\n\nfor n in 0::10 do\n    write_line(\"fibonacci({n}) = {fibonacci(n)}\");\nod",
